<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1864 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ja, vi elsker dette landet</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of Norway: words by Bjørnstjerne Bjørnson, music by his cousin Rikard Nordraak, first sung in public in 1864.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Bjørnstjerne Bjørnson</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Rikard Nordraak</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Norwegians; the Norwegian state</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Norwegian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Norway</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1859–1868; melody winter 1863–64; first sung in public 17 May 1864; officially adopted 11 December 2019</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Bjørnstjerne Bjørnson (written 1859–1868); music by Rikard Nordraak (winter 1863–64))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>For much of the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> had no single anthem. In the early part of the century many regarded &quot;Norges Skaal&quot; as one, and from 1820 the most recognised was &quot;Norsk Nationalsang&quot;, later known from its first words as &quot;Sønner av Norge&quot;, written by Henrik Anker Bjerregaard with a melody by Christian Blom after the Royal Norwegian Society for Development announced a competition for a national anthem in 1819.</p><p>Bjørnstjerne Bjørnson wrote &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ja-vi-elsker-dette-landet">Ja, vi elsker dette landet</a>&quot; (&quot;Yes, We Love This Country&quot;) between 1859 and 1868, and his cousin Rikard Nordraak composed the melody in the winter of 1863 to 1864. It was first performed in public on 17 May 1864, in connection with the 50th anniversary of the constitution. Bjørnson wrote in the form of Danish then used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>; the text sung today keeps his words with modernised spelling and punctuation, after the reforms of written <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bokmal">Bokmål</a>. The original version had a verse hailing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a>, who had succeeded his father as king of Norway in July 1859, and praising the &quot;brethren pact&quot;, a military treaty by which Norway, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> were to help one another if attacked. When German troops invaded South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> in February 1864, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a>, none of the partners came to Denmark's aid; the hope of a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> was shattered, Bjørnson turned from monarchist to republican, and the verse to the king was struck out.</p><p>The song came to be recognised as a national anthem from the mid-1860s, but until the early 20th century it was used alongside &quot;Sønner av Norge&quot;, which was preferred on official occasions. In 1905, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a>'s declaration of 7 June dissolved the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the two countries came close to war that autumn, Swedish socialists sang &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ja-vi-elsker-dette-landet">Ja, vi elsker dette landet</a>&quot; in support of the Norwegians' right to secede, while their leaders Hjalmar Branting and Zeth Höglund spoke for a peaceful settlement. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> both the Norwegian resistance and the Nazi collaborators used it, the collaborators mainly for propaganda, until the German occupiers forbade any use of the anthem.</p><p>Usually only the first and the last two verses (1, 7 and 8) are sung, the first by far the most often, and the last two lines of each verse are sung twice. In May 2006 the multicultural newspaper Utrop proposed an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> translation so that immigrants not yet fluent in Norwegian could honour their new country in song; a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a> called the idea &quot;integration in reverse&quot;, and one supporter of a translation received hate mail and threats. After the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> attacks, Ole Paus's &quot;Mitt lille land&quot; was sung at the memorial ceremonies and called &quot;a new national anthem&quot; by the media. &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ja-vi-elsker-dette-landet">Ja, vi elsker dette landet</a>&quot; was officially adopted as the national anthem on 11 December 2019. Norway also has an unofficial royal anthem, &quot;Kongesangen&quot;, and a &quot;national psalm&quot;, &quot;Gud signe vårt dyre fedreland&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of love for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, its rugged land and the homes of its people, which recalls the deeds and hardships of the nation's past and closes with a pledge to stand by the country and defend its peace. The mood is solemn and warm.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Norwegian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Norwegian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Ja, vi ælsker dette landet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yes, we love this land</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">som det stiger frem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as it rises forth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">furet, vejrbitt over vandet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>furrowed, weather-beaten, over the water,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">med de tusen hjæm, —</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with the thousand homes, —</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">ælsker, ælsker det, og tænker</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>love, love it, and think</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">på vor far og mor</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of our father and mother</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">og den saganat, som sænker</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the saga-night that lowers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">drømme på vor jord.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>dreams upon our earth.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Dette landet Harald bærget</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>This land Harald secured</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">med sin kjæmperad,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with his rank of champions,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">dette landet Håkon værget,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>this land Håkon defended,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">medens Øjvind kvad;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>while Øjvind sang;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Olav på det land har malet</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Olav upon that land has painted</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">korset med sit blod,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the cross with his blood,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">fra dets høje Sverre talet</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from its heights Sverre spoke</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Roma midt imod.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>right against Rome.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 64 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Bønder sine økser brynte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Peasants whetted their axes</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">hvor en hær drog frem;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>where an army marched forth;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Tordenskjold langs kysten lynte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tordenskjold flashed like lightning along the coast,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">så den lystes hjæm.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so that it was lighted home.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Kvinner selv stod op og strede,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Women themselves stood up and fought,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">som de vare mænn;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as if they were men;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">andre kunde bare græde,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>others could only weep,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">men det kom igjæn!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>but it came back again!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Visstnok var vi ikke mange;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Truly, we were not many;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">men vi strak dog til,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>but still we were enough,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">da vi prøvdes nogle gange,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>when we were tested a few times,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">og det stod på spill;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and it was at stake;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">ti vi heller landet brænte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for we would rather burn the land</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">æn det kom til fall;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>than let it come to its fall;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">husker bare, hvad som hændte</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>just remember what happened</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">ned på Fredrikshald!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>down at Fredrikshald!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Hårde tider har vi døjet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hard times we have endured,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">blev tilsist forstøtt;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>were at last cast off;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">men i værste nød blåøjet</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>but in the worst need, blue-eyed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">frihed blev os født.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>freedom was born to us.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Det gav faderkraft at bære</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That gave a father's strength to bear</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">hungersnød og krig,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>famine and war,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">det gav døden selv sin ære —</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it gave death itself its honour —</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">og det gav forlig.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and it gave reconciliation.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Fienden sit våben kastet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The enemy threw down his weapon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">op visiret fór,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>up the visor flew,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">vi med undren mot ham hastet;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we hastened towards him in wonder;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">ti han var vor bror.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for he was our brother.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Drevne frem på stand av skammen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Driven up onto our feet [?] by the shame,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">gik vi søderpå;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we went southward;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">nu vi står tre brødre sammen,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>now we stand three brothers together,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">og skal sådan stå!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and so shall we stand!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Norske mann i hus og hytte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Norseman in house and hut,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">takk din store Gud!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>thank your great God!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Landet vilde han beskytte,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The land he willed to protect,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">skjønt det mørkt så ud.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>though it looked dark.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Alt, hvad fædrene har kjæmpet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All that the fathers have fought for,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">mødrene har grætt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the mothers have wept for,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">har den Herre stille læmpet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Lord has quietly set right,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">så vi vant vor ret.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so that we won our right.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Ja, vi ælsker dette landet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yes, we love this land</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">som det stiger frem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as it rises forth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">furet, vejrbitt over vandet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>furrowed, weather-beaten, over the water,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">med de tusen hjæm.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with the thousand homes.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">Og som fædres kamp har hævet</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And as the fathers' struggle has raised</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">det av nød til sejr,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it from need to victory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">også vi, når det blir krævet,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we too, when it is demanded,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="no">for dets fred slår lejr.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>will pitch camp for its peace.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Bjørnstjerne Bjørnson (1859–1868), in his original spelling. Text from <a href="https://no.wikisource.org/wiki/Ja%2C_vi_%C3%A6lsker_dette_landet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (16)</summary><ul><li>line 1.2: 'som det stiger frem' is 'as it rises up, comes into view' (seen from the sea).</li><li>lines 1.7-1.8: 'saganat' is 'saga-night', the night of the old sagas, which 'sænker' (lets sink, lowers) dreams on the land.</li><li>line 2.1: 'bærget' (modern 'berget') can mean 'saved, secured' or 'gathered in' (as a harvest); here Harald gathering the land under his rule.</li><li>line 2.2: 'kjæmperad' is a row or rank of warriors, champions.</li><li>line 2.4: 'kvad' is 'chanted, sang (a poem)', as a skald; Øjvind is the skald Eyvind (Old Norse Eyvindr).</li><li>lines 2.7-2.8: 'talet Roma midt imod' is 'spoke out right against Rome', i.e. defied Rome.</li><li>line 3.4: 'så den lystes hjæm' is literally 'so that it was lighted home'; 'den' can be the army of line 3.2 (lit on its way home, sent back) or the coast; the sense is disputed.</li><li>line 3.8: 'men det kom igjæn' is 'but it came again': what came back is not named; it is usually taken as the land's fortune or freedom.</li><li>line 4.2: 'strak dog til' is 'were still enough, sufficed'.</li><li>line 4.8: Fredrikshald is the town now called Halden.</li><li>line 5.2: 'forstøtt' is 'cast off, rejected, disowned'.</li><li>line 5.8: 'forlig' is 'settlement, reconciliation'.</li><li>line 6.2: 'op visiret fór' is 'up went the visor' (of a helmet).</li><li>line 6.5: 'Drevne frem på stand av skammen' is hard to render: literally 'driven forth on stand by the shame'; read as 'roused to stand up (to take a stand) by the shame'.</li><li>line 7.7: 'læmpet' is 'eased, adjusted, gently set in order'.</li><li>line 8.8: 'slår lejr' is 'pitches camp, encamps': the people will take the field for the land's peace.</li></ul></details></section>
